---
export const title = 'Usage';
export const order = 100;
import DemoComponentCard from '../../../../components/DemoComponentCard.astro';
---

<p>
  A pie chart takes <code>data</code> as a list of slices, each with a unique <code>name</code>, a
  <code>value</code>, and an optional <code>label</code> and <code>color</code>. The pie is as large as the
  smaller of the container width and <code>height</code>, and the legend always shows. Hover a slice to pull it
  out and see its value and share. Keep it to about five slices; beyond that a bar chart reads better.
</p>

<p>
  Set <code>legend="hide"</code> to hide the legend. A pie has no hole: for one, with a centre label, use
  <code>wc-chart-doughnut</code> and its <code>inner-radius</code>.
</p>

<DemoComponentCard lang='html'>
  <wc-chart-pie id='pie-usage' label='Visitors by browser'></wc-chart-pie>
  <wc-button id='pie-usage-randomize' variant='tonal'>Randomize</wc-button>

  <script is:inline>
    const pieUsage = document.getElementById('pie-usage');
    pieUsage.data = [
      { name: 'chrome', label: 'Chrome', value: 275 },
      { name: 'safari', label: 'Safari', value: 200 },
      { name: 'firefox', label: 'Firefox', value: 187 },
      { name: 'edge', label: 'Edge', value: 173 },
      { name: 'other', label: 'Other', value: 90 },
    ];
    document.getElementById('pie-usage-randomize').addEventListener('click', () => {
      pieUsage.data = pieUsage.data.map(slice => ({
        ...slice,
        value: Math.round(Math.random() * 300) + 20,
      }));
    });
  </script>
</DemoComponentCard>
